@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@code{autoslip-howm} watches howm note creation through
@code{howm-create-file-hook}.  When a new note's title carries a
folgezettel address, the package writes a goto-link to the parent and a
matching goto-link in the parent that points back to the new note.

The package supports both org-flavored howm files (@file{.org}) and
plain-text howm files (@file{.txt}).  Heading style adapts to the file
flavor automatically.

@node Installation
@chapter Installation

@example
(add-to-list 'load-path "/path/to/autoslip-howm")
(require 'autoslip-howm)
(setq autoslip-howm-directory "~/howm/")
(autoslip-howm-mode 1)
@end example

@node Quick Start
@chapter Quick Start

Create a file whose first non-empty line begins with a folgezettel:

@example
1. Crystallography
@end example

Save the file.  Run @code{autoslip-howm-mode} and the package will add a
self-anchor.  Run @code{autoslip-howm-insert-next-child} to create a
child note; the package will write the bidirectional links.

@node Identity Model
@chapter Identity Model

Each note carries a self-anchor of the form

@example
<<< autoslip:1.2a:k7n3p3qr
@end example

The first segment is the namespace.  The second segment is the visible
folgezettel address.  The third segment is a stable UID minted at note
creation.  Inbound links use the goto marker:

@example
>>> autoslip:1.2a:k7n3p3qr
@end example

When a note is reparented, the address segment is rewritten in every
inbound goto-link, but the UID stays the same.  This makes reparenting
robust.

@node Address Format
@chapter Address Format

Identical to @code{autoslip-roam}.  The grammar is

@example
ROOT      ::= NUMBER "."
NUMBER    ::= [0-9]+
LETTERS   ::= [a-z]+
ADDRESS   ::= ROOT
            | ROOT NUMBER (LETTERS NUMBER?)*
@end example

@node Storage Modes
@chapter Storage Modes

The customization variable @code{autoslip-howm-link-storage} controls
where the package writes parent and child references.

@table @code
@item headings
The default.  Writes visible headings (@code{Parent Note} and
@code{Child Notes}) into the body of each note.

@item headers
Writes top-of-file header lines named by
@code{autoslip-howm-parent-property} and
@code{autoslip-howm-children-property}.  Use this when you want the body
of each note to remain free of automatic link text.
@end table

@node Index of Indices
@chapter Index of Indices

The roots of a folgezettel zettelkasten are the small set of major
topics that organize everything else.  In autoslip-howm those roots
have addresses like @code{1.}, @code{2.}, @code{3.}.  Each root anchors
a chain of thought that grows downward through child notes such as
@code{1.2}, @code{1.2a}, @code{1.2a3}.  A single visit to the vault
rarely shows every root at once.  The @code{00. Index of Indices} note
solves that problem.  It is a flat catalog of every root, and it lives
at a numerically lower address than @code{1.}, so it sorts to the top
of the tree view and to the top of any directory listing.

The package treats every @code{N.} address as a root with no parent.
The double-zero in @code{00.} is a sibling root, not a parent of
@code{1.}, @code{2.}, and the rest.  This is the right semantics,
because the index of indices is a pointer page, not a parent.

@section Workflow

Choose between five and twelve major topics, number them from 1, and
create a root for each before you create the index, because the index
must reference each root's stable wiki keyword.

@example
M-x autoslip-howm-create-note RET 1. RET Crystallography RET
M-x autoslip-howm-create-note RET 2. RET Statistical methods RET
@end example

With the roots in place, run @code{autoslip-howm-open-index}.  When
the index file does not yet exist, the command seeds it with a title
line, a self-anchor for @code{00.}, a top-level heading, and a sorted
list of every root.  Subsequent calls just open the file.

When you mint a new root later, refresh the auto-block with
@code{autoslip-howm-insert-root-list}, which writes a fresh,
folgezettel-sorted heading block at point.  The hand-written
annotations under each root entry are preserved in a separate section.

@section Anti-patterns

Do not give the real roots addresses like @code{00.1}, @code{00.2}.
That collapses the topology and breaks the parent-walk.  Keep the real
roots at @code{1.}, @code{2.}, @code{3.}.

Do not use @code{00.} as the only navigation surface.  The tree view
@code{autoslip-howm-show-tree} shows the full vault grouped under each
root and is more useful for browsing.  The index is the table of
contents.  The tree view is the table of contents plus every page
number.  Use both.

@node Hub and Topic Indexes
@chapter Hub and Topic Indexes

The folgezettel numbering system organizes each chain of thought
along one vertical axis, but related notes often sit in parallel
chains.  Two index shapes are supported, with deliberately different
wrapper headings so a reader can distinguish them at a glance.

@section Hub index

A hub index lists only the direct children of the current note.

@example
M-x autoslip-howm-insert-hub-index
@end example

The block is wrapped in a level-2 heading
@code{Children of N.M}, where @code{N.M} is the current note's
folgezettel.  Each child becomes a level-3 heading with a goto-link
on the line below.  Grandchildren and deeper descendants are
intentionally omitted.  Use this at the start or at a branching
point of a chain of thought.

@section Topic index

A topic index lists every note in the vault whose body matches a
regular expression.

@example
M-x autoslip-howm-insert-topic-index RET symmetry RET
@end example

The block is wrapped in a level-2 heading
@code{Topic Index: SEARCH-TERM}.  Each matching note becomes a
level-3 heading with a goto-link.  The current note is excluded
from its own topic index even when its body matches.  Use this for
cross-references that span chains of thought.

@section Visual distinction

The wrapper headings differ on purpose:

@example
** Children of 1.2a       <- hub index, vertical
** Topic Index: kinetics  <- topic index, horizontal
@end example

A reader scanning a hub note can tell at a glance which shape they
are looking at, and what scope to expect.

@section Search backends

Topic-index search ships with three backends, selected by
@code{autoslip-howm-search-backend}.

@table @code
@item auto
The default.  Use ripgrep when on PATH, then GNU or BSD grep, then
the in-Emacs scan.

@item rg
Always use ripgrep.  Fastest on large vaults (tens of thousands of
notes).  Regex flavor is rg's default (Rust regex, PCRE-like).

@item grep
Always use grep with @code{-E} (ERE flavor).  Portable across GNU
and BSD grep.

@item emacs
Always use the in-Emacs scan.  Slow on large vaults but supports
Emacs-flavor regex (@code{\\b}, @code{\\<}, @code{\\>}).
@end table

For literal words, character classes, anchors, alternation, and the
common quantifiers, all three flavors behave identically.  Subprocess
output is post-filtered through the cached note list, so hits inside
@code{.git}, backups, and any non-howm clutter under the howm
directory are dropped automatically.

@node Importing from Obsidian
@chapter Importing from Obsidian

The package can bootstrap a Howm zettelkasten from an existing
Obsidian @code{00. Index of Indices} note.  The Obsidian file is
expected to contain markdown links of the form
@code{[N. Title](N.%20Title.md)}, with a @code{## Project Support}
heading that splits a knowledge section from a project-management
section.

@example
M-x autoslip-howm-import-from-obsidian RET /path/to/file.md RET
@end example

For each entry, a Howm root note is created with the corresponding
folgezettel address.  Numbers in the project section are shifted by
@code{autoslip-howm-obsidian-project-offset} (default 400), so
Obsidian's @code{100. Research programs} becomes Howm's
@code{500. Research programs}.

@section Reconciliation

When a Howm @code{00.} index file already exists, the command prompts
for the master list (@code{obsidian}, @code{howm}, or @code{cancel}).
For each Obsidian entry, the importer tries to reuse an existing file
before minting a new one.  The dispatch is:

@enumerate
@item Address match.
A Howm note exists at the imported address.  If its title already
matches, the note is left alone.  Otherwise the master strategy
applies: @code{obsidian} rewrites the title; @code{howm} preserves it.

@item Title match.
No note has that address, but a note with the same title (compared
by slug after stripping any leading folgezettel) exists at a
different address or at no address at all.  Under
@code{obsidian}-master the existing note is renumbered to the
imported address: title line, self-anchor, inbound goto-links, and
file name are all rewritten in place.  Under @code{howm}-master the
existing note keeps its address and nothing new is created.

@item File on disk.
No cache match, but the canonical destination file already exists.
The importer adopts that file: ensures the first non-empty line reads
@code{ADDRESS TITLE} and that a self-anchor is present, while
leaving the rest of the body untouched.

@item Create.
None of the above.  A fresh root note is minted.
@end enumerate

The end-of-run message reports five counters: created, renamed,
renumbered, adopted, and unchanged.  The @code{00.} file body is
regenerated at the end so its root list is fresh.  Hand-written
annotations in the old @code{00.} file are not preserved by the
regeneration.

@node Importing Children from Obsidian
@chapter Importing Children from Obsidian

A separate command imports a markdown file that lists the children
of one parent note.

@example
M-x autoslip-howm-import-children-from-obsidian RET file.md RET 1. RET
@end example

The two prompts are the markdown source and the parent's
folgezettel address.  When the current buffer is already a tracked
howm note, that note's address is offered as the default parent.
Both standard markdown links @code{[N.M Title](url)} and Obsidian
wikilinks @code{[[N.M Title]]} are accepted.

For each entry the same four-tier reconciliation applies as for
the 00. import.  Entries whose address does not lie under the
parent are skipped silently, which keeps a master-node section in
the source file harmless.  After the dispatch, three things
happen.

@enumerate
@item Each child receives a backlink to the parent.
@item The parent receives a forward link to each child.
@item The parent receives one upward link to the 00. index, under
the heading named by @code{autoslip-howm-master-link-heading}.
@end enumerate

All three insertions are deduplicated, so re-running the command
after adding new children to the source file inserts only what was
missing.

To add an upward link to 00. from any note without running an
import, use @code{M-x autoslip-howm-add-master-link} directly.

@node Importing One Atomic Note from Obsidian
@chapter Importing One Atomic Note from Obsidian

For migrating one Obsidian note at a time with its body content
intact:

@example
M-x autoslip-howm-import-note-from-obsidian RET file.md RET
@end example

The file's base name is expected to begin with a folgezettel,
followed by a space and the title.  When present, a YAML front
matter block at the top of the file supplies the title (otherwise
the H1 is used, otherwise the file name).

The importer extracts four things:

@itemize
@item
@strong{Address} from the file name's leading token.
@item
@strong{Title} from YAML @code{title}, then the H1, then the file
name.
@item
@strong{Body} from everything between the H1 and the first
auto-managed section (@code{Parent Note}, @code{Child Notes},
@code{Related Notes}).  The Obsidian-managed sections themselves
are dropped because Howm regenerates them from cache.
@item
@strong{Parent address} from a wikilink inside the YAML
@code{source} field, falling back to the parent derived from the
imported address.
@end itemize

The same four-tier reconciliation runs.  The imported body is
then written into the body region of the resulting Howm note,
following @code{autoslip-howm-import-note-body-strategy} (default
@code{'replace}).  When the parent note exists in the Howm vault,
a bidirectional link is set up between the imported note and its
parent.

Tags, @code{date-created}, and the Obsidian-managed sections in
the source file are not carried into Howm.  Add them by hand if
they matter.

@node Bulk Import from Finder or a Directory
@chapter Bulk Import from Finder or a Directory

Two commands wrap the single-note importer for batch migration.

@section From a Finder selection (macOS)

@example
M-x autoslip-howm-import-notes-from-finder-selection
@end example

Calls @code{osascript} to read Finder's current selection, filters
the list to files ending in @code{.md}, asks for confirmation, and
routes each file through the single-atomic-note path.  Requires
macOS; the command refuses to run when @code{osascript} is not on
PATH.  The AppleScript snippet is exposed as
@code{autoslip-howm-finder-selection-applescript} for
customization.

In this bulk path the @code{prompt} value of
@code{autoslip-howm-import-note-body-strategy} silently falls back
to @code{replace} to avoid asking once per file.  Per-file
failures are collected in @code{*Autoslip-Howm Bulk Import
Errors*} and do not abort the run.

@section From a directory

@example
M-x autoslip-howm-import-notes-from-directory RET dir RET
@end example

Cross-platform alternative.  Non-recursive by default; a prefix
argument makes the search descend into subdirectories.

@node Commands
@chapter Commands

@table @code
@item autoslip-howm-mode
Toggle the global minor mode.

@item autoslip-howm-create-note
Create a note at an arbitrary address.  Prompts for the address and
title, writes the file with a leading-address filename, drops the
self-anchor, and visits the new buffer.  Use this to mint roots
(@code{1.}, @code{2.}, ...) and any note whose parent is not the
current buffer.

@item autoslip-howm-insert-next-child
Create a child note of the current note.  Suggests the next available
address, prompts for the title, and wires the bidirectional links.

@item autoslip-howm-add-backlink-to-parent
Add bidirectional links manually.

@item autoslip-howm-goto-parent
Visit the parent note.

@item autoslip-howm-list-children
Pick a direct child and visit it.

@item autoslip-howm-show-tree
Display the whole vault as a folgezettel-ordered tree.

@item autoslip-howm-open-index
Visit the index-of-indices file, creating it if absent.  When it does
not yet exist, seeds it with a self-anchor for @code{00.} and a sorted
block of every root note.

@item autoslip-howm-insert-root-list
Insert a folgezettel-sorted heading block of every root at point.
Each root becomes a level-2 heading, and a goto-link to the root's
wiki keyword appears on the line below.  Heading style adapts to the
file flavor (org-mode versus plain-text howm).

@item autoslip-howm-insert-hub-index
Insert an index of the direct children of the current note.  The
block is wrapped in a level-2 @code{Children of N.M} heading.  Each
child becomes a level-3 heading with a goto-link.  Grandchildren are
not included.

@item autoslip-howm-insert-topic-index
Insert an index of every note whose body matches a regular
expression.  The block is wrapped in a level-2
@code{Topic Index: SEARCH-TERM} heading.  Each match becomes a
level-3 heading with a goto-link.  The current note is excluded from
its own topic index.

@item autoslip-howm-import-from-obsidian
Import a 00. markdown index file from Obsidian.  Creates a Howm root
note for each entry, remaps project-support numbers, and reconciles
with any existing Howm @code{00.} file under a master-list prompt.

@item autoslip-howm-import-children-from-obsidian
Import a markdown child list under a parent.  Prompts for the
markdown path and the parent's folgezettel address.  Wires up
parent backlinks, forward links, and a master-node link from the
parent up to 00.  Idempotent.

@item autoslip-howm-import-note-from-obsidian
Import a single atomic Obsidian note.  Parses YAML front matter,
H1, and body; runs the four-tier reconciliation; writes the body
into the Howm note's body region; wires the bidirectional link to
the parent.

@item autoslip-howm-import-notes-from-finder-selection
Bulk-import every Markdown file currently selected in macOS
Finder.  Requires macOS and osascript on PATH.

@item autoslip-howm-import-notes-from-directory
Bulk-import every Markdown file under a directory.  Cross-platform.
With a prefix argument, descends into subdirectories.

@item autoslip-howm-add-master-link
Insert an upward link from the current note to the 00. index of
indices.  Idempotent.

@item autoslip-howm-rebuild-child-notes
Replace the Child Notes section of the current note with a fresh
folgezettel-sorted listing.  Each entry becomes a level-3 heading
carrying the child's full title, followed by a goto-link on the
next line.  Useful for upgrading parent files written by older
versions of the package, where forward links were bare goto-lines
without titles.

@item autoslip-howm-follow-link-at-point
Visit the note named by the autoslip keyword on the current line.
Recognizes both `>>>` goto-links and `<<<` self-anchors.  When
`autoslip-howm-mode' is enabled the command is also wired into
Org's @code{org-open-at-point} via
@code{org-open-at-point-functions}, so @kbd{C-c C-o} on a keyword
line follows the link inside Org-mode index files.

@item autoslip-howm-show-chain-of-thought
Show the ancestor chain in a buffer.

@item autoslip-howm-insert-chain-of-thought
Insert the ancestor chain at point as a bullet list.

@item autoslip-howm-reparent
Move the current note to a new folgezettel address.

@item autoslip-howm-reparent-subtree
Move the current note and every descendant.

@item autoslip-howm-rescan
Rebuild the in-memory note cache.
@end table

@node Customization
@chapter Customization

All variables are in the @code{autoslip-howm} customization group.

@table @code
@item autoslip-howm-directory
The howm directory to scan.

@item autoslip-howm-default-extension
Either @code{".org"} or @code{".txt"}.

@item autoslip-howm-index-file-name
File name used by @code{autoslip-howm-open-index}.  Defaults to
@code{"00.-index-of-indices.org"}.

@item autoslip-howm-obsidian-project-heading-regexp
Regexp matching the heading that splits knowledge roots from
project-support roots in an imported Obsidian index file.  Defaults
to @code{"^##[ \t]+Project[ \t]+Support[ \t]*$"}.

@item autoslip-howm-obsidian-project-offset
Integer offset added to project-section root numbers on import.
Default 400 maps Obsidian's 100, 101, 102 onto Howm's 500, 501, 502.

@item autoslip-howm-hub-index-heading-format
Format string for the wrapper heading inserted by
@code{autoslip-howm-insert-hub-index}.  Default
@code{"Children of %s"}.

@item autoslip-howm-topic-index-heading-format
Format string for the wrapper heading inserted by
@code{autoslip-howm-insert-topic-index}.  Default
@code{"Topic Index: %s"}.

@item autoslip-howm-master-link-heading
Heading used above the upward link inserted by
@code{autoslip-howm-add-master-link} and the children-import
command.  Default @code{"Master Node"}.  Set to nil to insert the
link without a heading.

@item autoslip-howm-import-note-body-strategy
How @code{autoslip-howm-import-note-from-obsidian} writes the
imported body when a Howm file already exists for the address.
One of @code{'replace} (default), @code{'append}, or
@code{'prompt}.

@item autoslip-howm-search-backend
Search backend used by topic-index queries.  One of @code{'auto},
@code{'rg}, @code{'grep}, or @code{'emacs}.  Default @code{'auto}
picks the fastest available tool.

@item autoslip-howm-rg-program
Name or absolute path of the ripgrep executable.  Default
@code{"rg"}.

@item autoslip-howm-grep-program
Name or absolute path of the grep executable.  Default
@code{"grep"}.

@item autoslip-howm-link-storage
Either @code{'headings} or @code{'headers}.

@item autoslip-howm-rename-files-on-reparent
Whether to rename files on disk when reparenting.

@item autoslip-howm-anchor-marker, autoslip-howm-goto-marker
Howm wiki markers, defaulting to @code{<<<} and @code{>>>}.
@end table

@node Reparenting
@chapter Reparenting

@code{autoslip-howm-reparent} rewrites the title line, rewrites the
self-anchor's address segment, renames the file on disk, and rewrites
every inbound keyword reference across the vault.  Because the UID
segment never changes, the rewrite is collision-free.

@code{autoslip-howm-reparent-subtree} applies the rename to every
descendant of the current note.

@node Phased Implementation Status
@chapter Phased Implementation Status

This release is the Phase 1 scaffold.  Phases 2 through 4 (cache,
link writing, navigation) are implemented to a working baseline.
Phase 5 (chain-of-thought and cross-linked chains buffers with their
own major modes and insert-back actions) is still pending.

